WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.360
The thing on the top left here: a Taktia router.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:11.480
These guys from MIT. They made a handheld router

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.740
that's a little robotic computer-controlled router.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:18.480
So it's a little handheld router
like you'd use in woodworking.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.326
But the router itself can move a couple inches

00:00:20.326 --> 00:00:22.420
back and forth within the little case that you hold.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.000
It's got a little camera on it

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.960
it's got a little screen. And what it does

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:27.440
is you can actually say I want to

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:29.220
cut a straight line from here to here.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:32.940
And as long as you keep the dot in the circle
it will adjust the

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:36.630
router to take all your jaggy motions out. And just cut

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:39.760
a perfectly straight line. Or I want
to cut a star shape out

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.560
and I don't have the skill to actually do that by hand.

00:00:42.566 --> 00:00:43.829
But the machine will totally help you do

00:00:43.829 --> 00:00:47.579
the fine motor control to make that work.
So I think we're going to see a

00:00:47.579 --> 00:00:51.660
lot more stuff like that. Where it's not
about making some machine like we have.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.320
Like we have a giant robot that does the
exact same thing. And I don't have

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.360
to keep a dot in a circle or anything.
I program it, tell it what to do, walk away.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:02.879
It cuts stuff while I'm off working.
But in this instance anybody can

00:01:02.879 --> 00:01:05.489
pick that up and use it. You don't have to
program it, you don't have to know CAD...

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:10.960
That kind of semi-automated approach
is just huge.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:16.500
A great thing on the top right there, too, is
people working alongside robots.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.000
So a robot arm like this one,
that's carving out foam, it will kill you.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.860
If you stand next to it. If it swings over
while you're working next to it will...

00:01:24.869 --> 00:01:27.930
it will cause a lot of damage to you.
They're big. They're heavy. They're very

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:32.070
powerful. So this thing has to be in a
cage by OSHA rules. And you can't go

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:35.360
anywhere near it when it's working.
But that robot up there at the top,

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:39.700
that's a special robot that this new company
called Universal Robotics is making.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:44.560
It's all belt-driven internally.
If that robot arm hits you it doesn't hurt you at all.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.140
It just hits you and that's it. It has a slip belt system

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:50.240
on the inside. So if it hits something
it doesn't do any damage.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:51.520
It just stops.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:54.920
So that arm is what's loading
and unloading that CNC mill

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:58.860
that that machinist is working at. 
So instead of the machinist wasting his time

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.940
loading and unloading the mill, the
robot arm loads and unloads the mill.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:07.469
And that guy can do higher value tasks.
So semi-automated. I'm not trying to

00:02:07.469 --> 00:02:11.340
make it to where nobody's ever needed.
But I'm keeping a human in the loop

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.030
because humans are awesome and they do great jobs.

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:17.200
But at the same time I'm working
side-by-side with them.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.220
The other thing is that--a friend of ours
that does big massive sculptures, he did the

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:25.349
bow and arrow on the waterfront here in
San Francisco. His name is Bill Chrysler, he's

00:02:25.349 --> 00:02:30.180
a really awesome guy. He has a CNC
machine the size of a basketball court.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.750
It wouldn't fit in this room. It's a
five-axis machine. He bought it from

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:38.370
a boat builder its massive. Massive thing.
For a lot of their projects, when they're

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:41.720
doing complicated sculptural work,
they'll actually have the CNC router

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:45.740
only get it about 80% of the way there.
And then their talented crew of sculptors

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:48.420
comes in and finishes the 20% by hand.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:53.360
Because it's faster. It's that 80/20 rule.
The machine can do 80%

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:56.340
of the work in 20% of the
time. And then that last little bit of

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:59.160
finishing work is done by hand because
that's where the artistry and the craft

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:03.140
really come into it to make the piece really
good. Like if you tried to make it to

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:05.920
where it came off the CNC machine 100% done

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.020
you're going to spend a 100x the amount of time

00:03:09.023 --> 00:03:10.799
in programming the CNC machine. And it

00:03:10.799 --> 00:03:14.040
still might not look as good.
Because it didn't have that final touch that a

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:21.930
a talented sculptor's able to do.
Lastly, you know, this is a protein folding

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:25.560
diagram. One of the cool things is when
you start to make things with robots

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:29.040
is you can make things--the joke that we 
say is--you can make things that want

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.040
to be built. So if you put all the parts in a box
and you shook the box long enough

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.440
you'd wind up with the assembled cabinet
like what's under the stairs over here.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:38.940
It's a theoretical idea, but basically we can

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:42.560
make it so that stuff only goes
together the right way. So again it's

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:45.740
semi-automated. I'm not trying to make a
machine that will assemble cabinets for me.

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:48.840
Instead I'm making really smart parts
that are really simple for anybody to

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.409
put together to make a cabinet.

00:03:50.409 --> 00:03:52.640
So everything's kind of IKEA-like or something.

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:55.560
And nature is a great example of that.

